# Migration Guidelines

### Database Migration Rules

#### When to Check These Rules
- When creating new migrations
- When modifying existing migrations
- When adding or modifying database columns
- When creating or modifying indexes

#### Rules
- Always use the Rails Rake task to generate migrations
- Never manually create migration files
- Use the format: `bin/rails generate migration [MigrationName] [column:type]`
- Let Rails handle the timestamp and migration number
- Include proper indexes for foreign keys
- Use reversible migrations when possible
- Add appropriate validations in models
- Consider performance implications

#### Examples
✅ Correct:
```bash
bin/rails generate migration AddNameToUsers name:string
bin/rails generate migration AddIndexToUsersEmail email:string:index
```

✅ Correct Migration:
```ruby
class AddNameToUsers < ActiveRecord::Migration[7.0]
  def change
    add_column :users, :name, :string
    add_index :users, :email, unique: true
  end
end
```

❌ Incorrect:
- Manually creating migration files
- Not using proper column types
- Missing indexes for foreign keys
- Not making migrations reversible
- Not considering performance

#### Common Mistakes to Avoid
- Manual migration file creation
- Missing indexes
- Not using proper column types
- Not making migrations reversible
- Not considering performance impact

#### Validation Checklist
- [ ] Using Rails generator
- [ ] Proper column types
- [ ] Appropriate indexes
- [ ] Reversible migrations
- [ ] Performance considerations
- [ ] Model validations added
